@page "/"
@model IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = null;
    ViewData["Description"] = "Write one post six ways, attach an image to each version, and publish or schedule them all at once.";
}

<section class="hero">
    <h1>One post. Six versions. One click.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        I got tired of writing the same thought five times, trimming it for X, padding it for
        LinkedIn, then forgetting Mastodon entirely. SocialShare lets me write each version once,
        in one screen, and send them all at the same moment.
    </p>
    <div class="row" style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
        @if (Model.RegistrationEnabled)
        {
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/register">Create account</a>
        }
        <a class="btn" href="/login">Sign in</a>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="stack" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-7);">
    <h2>The six it posts to</h2>
    <ul class="platform-list">
        @foreach (var platform in Model.Platforms)
        {
            <li>@platform.DisplayName</li>
        }
    </ul>
    <p class="muted measure">
        Each one gets its own body and its own featured image. Leave a platform blank and it gets
        skipped. Every body is counted against that platform's real limit as you type, and the
        server checks it again before anything goes out.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="stack" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-7);">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
        <li>
            <h3>Connect your accounts</h3>
            <p class="muted small">
                You bring your own developer app for each platform and paste the client id and
                secret in. Bluesky just needs an app password. Nothing is shared between users.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3>Write the versions</h3>
            <p class="muted small">
                Start from a master draft and copy it into the platforms you want, then edit each
                one. Attach an image per platform, with the recommended size right next to it.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3>Publish or schedule</h3>
            <p class="muted small">
                Send it now, or pick a date and time in your own time zone. A background worker
                picks scheduled posts up and publishes them without you being logged in.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3>See what happened</h3>
            <p class="muted small">
                Every platform reports back separately. Success gets a link to the live post,
                failure gets the actual error text and a retry button. Partial success is normal.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section class="grid" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-7);">
    <div class="card">
        <h3>Scheduling that survives a restart</h3>
        <p class="muted small">
            A scheduled post is claimed before it publishes, so a deploy in the middle of a send
            cannot post it twice. Failed platforms retry three times with backoff and then wait
            for you.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h3>Your credentials stay yours</h3>
        <p class="muted small">
            No platform passwords are stored, because none of these APIs accept one. Tokens and
            app passwords are encrypted before they touch the database, and the app itself holds
            no platform credentials of its own.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h3>Honest about the limits</h3>
        <p class="muted small">
            X needs a paid developer tier to post at all. Instagram will not take a text only
            post. Mastodon's character limit depends on the instance. All three are handled and
            all three are said out loud.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="stack" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-7);">
    <h2>What it looks like</h2>
    <p class="muted measure">
        One tab per platform, each with its own body, live counter and image. The master draft at
        the top is optional. These are screenshots of the real thing.
    </p>

    <figure style="margin:0;">
        <img src="~/img/compose.png"
             alt="The compose screen with a tab for each of the six platforms, the Bluesky tab selected, a body with a live character counter reading 186 of 300, and an attached featured image with alt text."
             width="1600" height="1044"
             style="border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);" />
        <figcaption class="small muted" style="margin-top:var(--space-2);">
            The compose screen, with the Bluesky version selected.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure style="margin:0;">
        <img src="~/img/dashboard.png"
             alt="The dashboard showing scheduled posts with their times and platforms, and recently published posts with a per platform status."
             width="1600" height="777"
             style="border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);" />
        <figcaption class="small muted" style="margin-top:var(--space-2);">
            The dashboard. What is queued up, and what already went out.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section class="card">
    <h2>Worth knowing before you sign up</h2>
    <ul class="muted small measure">
        <li>You need your own developer app for LinkedIn, X, Threads, Instagram and, indirectly, Mastodon. Bluesky needs nothing but an app password.</li>
        <li>Posting to X requires a paid tier on your X developer project. Connecting works on the free tier, posting does not.</li>
        <li>Instagram needs a Business or Creator account linked to a Facebook Page, and every Instagram post needs an image.</li>
        <li>Uploaded images are served from a public but unguessable URL, because Threads and Instagram fetch images rather than accept uploads.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">
        @if (Model.RegistrationEnabled)
        {
            <a href="/register">Create an account</a><text> and the setup guide walks each of those through step by step.</text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>Sign ups are closed on this instance. If you have an account, the setup guide walks each of those through step by step once you are signed in.</text>
        }
    </p>
</section>
